{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** The ankle is a synovial hinge joint between the lower end of the tibia, both malleoli and the talus. The tibia, malleoli and inferior transverse tibiofibular ligament form a deep socket (mortise). Its movements are dorsiflexion and plantar flexion. The strong medial (deltoid) ligament and the weaker three-band lateral ligament stabilise it.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="AN20.1" phase="Phase 1" /%}

## What kind of joint is the ankle?

The ankle (talocrural) joint is a **synovial joint of the hinge variety**. The talus moves on a transverse axis, like a hinge. The ankle is the transition zone between the leg and the foot. It allows a wide range of movement but also has to transmit large forces.

It is the most commonly injured joint, and ligament sprains and tears are particularly common. Even so, the shape of the bones, the strength of the ligaments and the surrounding tendons make it strong and stable.

A **malleolus** is the bony projection at each side of the ankle: the medial malleolus belongs to the tibia and the lateral malleolus to the fibula. The **mortise** is the socket they form together, and the name compares the joint to a tenon fitting into a mortise in carpentry.

## Articular surfaces

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/ankle-joint/ankle-bones-mortise.webp" alt="Bones of the ankle forming the mortise around the talus." caption="The tibia and both malleoli form a socket for the talus." credit="OpenStax College, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY 3.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:919_Ankle_Feet_Joints.jpg" width="960" height="1087" /%}

**Upper (socket) surface**, formed by three structures:

- The lower end of the tibia, including the medial malleolus
- The lateral malleolus of the fibula
- The inferior transverse tibiofibular ligament, which bridges the gap between tibia and fibula behind the talus

Together these make a deep socket.

**Lower surface:** articular areas on the upper, medial and lateral aspects of the body of the talus.

All articular surfaces are covered with hyaline cartilage. The fibula takes no part in the knee joint, but it does take part in the ankle joint.

The talus is **wedge-shaped**, wider in front, and the malleoli are oriented to fit this wedge. The talus has no muscular attachment.

## Capsule and synovial membrane

- The **fibrous capsule** surrounds the joint. It is thin and weak in front and behind, which allows hinge movement. On each side it is supported by strong collateral ligaments.
- It is attached all around the articular margins with two exceptions. Posterosuperiorly it is attached to the inferior transverse tibiofibular ligament. Anteroinferiorly it is attached to the dorsum of the neck of the talus, some distance from the trochlear surface. So the proximal part of the dorsal talar surface lies inside the capsule.
- The **synovial membrane** lines the capsule. The joint cavity ascends for a short distance between the tibia and fibula, and the membrane projects into the lower part of the inferior tibiofibular joint.

## Ligaments

{% table %}
- **Ligament**
- **Attachments and key points**
- **What it does**
---
- **Medial (deltoid) ligament**
- A strong triangular band. The apex and margins of the medial malleolus are the common upper attachment. Superficial fibres: tibionavicular (navicular tuberosity and the spring ligament), tibiocalcanean (whole length of the sustentaculum tali) and posterior tibiotalar (medial tubercle of the talus). A deep anterior tibiotalar part goes to the medial surface of the talus. Crossed by the tendons of tibialis posterior and flexor digitorum longus.
- Resists eversion of the hindfoot; the deep part prevents external rotation of the talus
---
- **Anterior talofibular (ATFL)**
- From the lateral malleolus to the lateral surface (neck) of the talus. Runs almost horizontally. Relaxed in dorsiflexion, tense in plantar flexion.
- Weakest point in the inverted, plantar-flexed foot
---
- **Calcaneofibular**
- A long rounded cord from the tip of the lateral malleolus downward and backward to the lateral surface of the calcaneum. Crossed by the tendons of peroneus longus and brevis.
- Tight in inversion and dorsiflexion; also helps stabilise the subtalar joint
---
- **Posterior talofibular**
- From the lateral malleolus (malleolar fossa) to the posterior part of the talus
- Posterior stability; almost always spared in inversion sprains
---
- **Inferior transverse tibiofibular**
- Thick band of yellow elastic fibres, the deep part of the posterior tibiofibular ligament. It projects below the bones and touches the talus.
- Deepens the socket behind the talus
{% /table %}

The **lateral ligament is weaker than the medial ligament** and has three bands (ATFL, calcaneofibular, posterior talofibular). The deltoid is so strong that excessive tensile force tends to cause an **avulsion fracture** (a piece of bone pulled off) rather than a tear of the ligament.

The inferior extensor retinaculum, the peroneal retinacula and the interosseous tibiofibular ligament also contribute to stability.

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Lateral ligament in one line:** three bands from the lateral malleolus, to the talus in front (ATFL), the calcaneum in the middle (calcaneofibular) and the talus behind (posterior talofibular). The front band is the one most often torn.
{% /callout %}

## The inferior tibiofibular joint (syndesmosis)

The lower ends of the tibia and fibula are joined by a **syndesmosis**: a fibrous joint held by ligaments only. It has **no capsule**. Four ligaments hold it:

- Anterior tibiofibular ligament
- Posterior tibiofibular ligament (stronger than the anterior)
- Inferior transverse ligament
- Interosseous ligament, a thickened part of the interosseous membrane. It is the strongest union between the two bones.

This complex still permits some movement at the tibiofibular joint during dorsiflexion and plantar flexion. The fibula has no direct contact with the weight-bearing part of the talus, yet the syndesmosis transmits about 16% of the axial load to the fibula.

## What keeps the ankle stable?

The joint is structurally very strong. Stability comes from:

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/ankle-joint/ankle-lateral-ligaments.webp" alt="Lateral view of the ankle showing the three lateral ligaments." caption="The anterior talofibular ligament is the one most often torn in sprains." credit="Laboratoires Servier, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 3.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lateral_collateral_ligament_of_ankle_joint.png" width="960" height="687" /%}

1. **Close interlocking** of the articular surfaces: the wedge-shaped talus sits in the deep mortise.
2. **Strong collateral ligaments** on both sides.
3. **Tendons crossing the joint:** four in front, three on the posteromedial side and two on the posterolateral side.

Two forces tend to displace the tibia and fibula forward over the talus: the forward pull of the tendons passing from leg to foot, and the pull of gravity when the heel is raised. They are resisted by the wedge shape of the talus, the downward prolongation of the posterior border of the tibia, the inferior transverse ligament, and the ligaments that pass backward (the tibiocalcanean and posterior tibiotalar parts of the deltoid, the calcaneofibular and the posterior talofibular).

## Movements and muscles (AN20.1)

The primary movements are **dorsiflexion** (toes up) and **plantar flexion** (toes down). **Inversion and eversion do not happen at the ankle.** They occur mainly at the subtalar and other tarsal joints.

{% table %}
- **Movement**
- **Principal muscles**
- **Accessory muscles**
---
- **Dorsiflexion**
- Tibialis anterior
- Extensor digitorum longus, extensor hallucis longus, peroneus (fibularis) tertius
---
- **Plantar flexion**
- Gastrocnemius, soleus
- Plantaris, tibialis posterior, flexor hallucis longus, flexor digitorum longus
{% /table %}

Key points on movement:

- **Dorsiflexion is the close-packed position.** The wider front part of the talus fits into the narrow part of the socket, so the surfaces fit most closely and the ligaments are tight. There is no chance of dislocation in dorsiflexion. Dorsiflexion is limited by the tension of the tendo calcaneus, the posterior fibres of the medial ligament and the calcaneofibular ligament.
- **Plantar flexion is the less stable position.** The ligaments of the distal tibiofibular joint are less taut, so small amounts of rotation, abduction and adduction become possible.
- The talus is wider in front, so in dorsiflexion the malleolar gap widens a little. The fibula rotates slightly, the syndesmosis gives, and there is gliding at the superior tibiofibular joint.
- The axis of rotation is not fixed. It shifts during movement, so the joint is only approximately uniaxial.
- **Range:** values vary between references and are approximate. One gives about 20 degrees of dorsiflexion and 45 degrees of plantar flexion from the right-angle position. Another gives 10 degrees of dorsiflexion with the knee straight, 30 degrees with the knee flexed (when the calcaneal tendon is relaxed), and 30 degrees of plantar flexion. Learn the version your department teaches.
- **In walking,** plantar flexors raise the heel from the ground. When the limb swings forward, the dorsiflexors help the foot clear the ground. The value of the joint lies in this to and fro hinge action.

## Relations

All the tendons crossing the ankle lie **superficial to the capsule** and are enclosed in synovial sheaths for part of their course.

- **Anterior, from medial to lateral:** tibialis anterior, extensor hallucis longus, anterior tibial vessels, deep fibular nerve, extensor digitorum longus, fibularis tertius.
- **Posteromedial, from medial to lateral:** tibialis posterior, flexor digitorum longus, posterior tibial vessels, tibial nerve, flexor hallucis longus.
- **Posterolateral (groove behind the lateral malleolus):** fibularis longus and brevis. Brevis lies in front of longus at this level.
- **Posterior:** the tendo calcaneus (Achilles tendon), with plantaris.
- The **great (long) saphenous vein and saphenous nerve** cross in front of the medial malleolus, medial to the tibialis anterior tendon. The nerve lies behind the vein.

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Posteromedial order:** tendon, tendon, vessels, nerve, tendon. The two tendons are tibialis posterior and flexor digitorum longus. The vessels and nerve are the posterior tibial vessels and the tibial nerve. The last tendon is flexor hallucis longus.
{% /callout %}

**Surface anatomy:** the lateral malleolus descends to a more distal level than the medial malleolus and lies on a more posterior plane. The tip of the medial malleolus is about 1.3 cm proximal to the tip of the lateral malleolus. The posterior tibial artery can be felt halfway between the medial malleolus and the heel.

## Blood supply, nerve supply and lymph

- **Arteries:** malleolar branches of the anterior tibial, posterior tibial and fibular (peroneal) arteries.
- **Nerves:** the deep fibular, saphenous, sural and tibial nerves (or the medial and lateral plantar nerves, depending on the level of division of the tibial nerve). Occasionally the superficial fibular nerve also supplies the joint.
- **Lymph:** by vessels accompanying the arteries, and superficially along the long and small saphenous veins.

The inferior tibiofibular joint has its own supply. Its arteries are the perforating branch of the fibular artery and the lateral malleolar branches of the anterior and posterior tibial arteries. Its nerves are branches of the deep fibular and sural nerves.

## Applied anatomy: sprains

A **sprain** is a stretch or tear of a ligament. A partial tear leaves some of the ligament intact, and once healed it can stabilise the joint. A complete tear may heal without regaining its original form and length, so the joint can stay unstable.

- **Lateral ankle sprain (common):** the foot is plantar flexed and excessively inverted. The ATFL is torn first, then the calcaneofibular ligament. The posterior talofibular ligament is almost always spared. In more than 75% of ankle ligament injuries the lateral complex is involved. Torn fibres bleed into the soft tissues, giving pain and swelling. Sometimes the tip of the malleolus is avulsed, a peroneal tendon is injured, or the base of the fifth metatarsal is fractured.
- **Why the ATFL?** In plantar flexion it changes from nearly horizontal to nearly vertical and is at greatest stretch. This explains injuries from a pothole or a kerb.
- **Medial ankle sprain (less common):** excessive eversion strains the deltoid ligament. Because the deltoid is so strong, medial ligament injuries are usually associated with a fracture or joint injury.
- **High ankle sprain:** injury to the syndesmosis. In the **syndesmosis squeeze test**, squeezing the upper lower leg causes pain at the ankle.

**Clinical tests for lateral instability:**

- **Talar tilt test:** with the ankle neutral, the examiner stabilises the tibia and forces the heel into maximum inversion. A tilt of 15 degrees, or 5 degrees more than the normal side, is abnormal. Inversion laxity suggests injury to both the calcaneofibular ligament and the ATFL.
- **Anterior drawer test:** the patient sits with the knee flexed to 90 degrees and the ankle in 10 degrees of plantar flexion. The examiner forces the heel forward under the tibia. In a positive test the talus slides forward.

**Initial treatment:** the PRICE approach for the first 48 to 72 hours, to limit bleeding and swelling, then rehabilitation as soon as possible.

## Applied anatomy: fractures and dislocation

- **Dislocation is rare** because of the deep tibiofibular socket. When it happens it is accompanied by a fracture of one of the malleoli.
- Injuries are grouped by five mechanisms: **adduction, abduction, pronation-external rotation, supination-external rotation and vertical compression.** The fracture pattern depends on the position of the foot and the direction of the force. A malleolus that is pushed off usually fractures obliquely. One that is pulled off fractures transversely.
- **Adduction (inversion) injury:** with the foot plantar flexed it sprains the lateral ligament. With the ankle neutral or dorsiflexed it can fracture the medial malleolus obliquely.
- **Abduction (eversion) injury:** the medial side is pulled apart, so the deltoid ruptures or the medial malleolus is avulsed. The lateral malleolus is compressed and fractures at the level of the mortise.
- The "invisible" ligament part of an ankle injury can be as serious as the fracture, so always assess the ligaments when a malleolus is broken.
- **Talar neck fractures:** about 70% of the talar surface is covered by articular cartilage. A displaced talar neck fracture can interrupt the blood supply to the talar body and lead to avascular necrosis and non-union.

## Imaging and joint access

- The **Ottawa Ankle Rules** can rule out fractures without radiography in suitable patients. Check for bony tenderness, then ask the patient to bear weight for four steps.
- If an X-ray is needed, the standard views are anteroposterior, lateral and mortise. About 95% of ankle fractures are detected with any two of the three trauma-series views. MRI is useful for tendons and soft tissues.
- **Injection into the joint:** the needle goes between the tendons of extensor hallucis longus and tibialis anterior, with the ankle partly plantar flexed.
- If the ankle is going to stiffen (ankylosis), the best position to end up in is slight plantar flexion.

## Tendons around the ankle

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/ankle-joint/ankle-tendons-retinacula.webp" alt="Tendons passing around the ankle joint." caption="Tendons cross the ankle but do not form part of the joint." credit="Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons" license="Public domain" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gray441.png" width="700" height="488" /%}

- The **Achilles tendon** is the largest tendon in the body. It has no tendon sheath, but the tissue around it can become inflamed (paratenonitis).
- **Tibialis posterior** is the strongest of the posteromedial tendons and the most often affected by disease. With the spring ligament it maintains the medial arch, so a tear can lead to flat foot.
- The peroneal tendons can dislocate from the groove behind the lateral malleolus, sometimes together with an ankle sprain.

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="The upper articular surface is the lower end of the tibia with the medial malleolus, the lateral malleolus of the fibula, and the inferior transverse tibiofibular ligament. Together they form a deep socket for the talus." %}
Which structure, with the tibia and the lateral malleolus, completes the deep socket of the ankle joint?
- A. Anterior talofibular ligament
- B. Inferior transverse tibiofibular ligament
- C. Spring ligament
- D. Interosseous membrane only
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="Dorsiflexion is the close-packed position. The wider front of the talus fits the narrow part of the socket and the ligaments are tight, so dislocation does not occur. In plantar flexion the joint is less stable." %}
The ankle joint is most stable in:
- A. Full plantar flexion
- B. Inversion
- C. Full dorsiflexion
- D. Eversion
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="In plantar flexion the anterior talofibular ligament turns from nearly horizontal to nearly vertical and is stretched most. An inversion injury with the foot plantar flexed therefore tears it first, followed by the calcaneofibular ligament." %}
A footballer lands on an inverted, plantar-flexed foot. Which ligament is injured first?
- A. Anterior talofibular ligament
- B. Posterior talofibular ligament
- C. Deltoid ligament
- D. Inferior transverse ligament
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="Inversion and eversion occur mainly at the subtalar and other tarsal joints. The ankle itself is a hinge that allows dorsiflexion and plantar flexion." %}
Inversion and eversion of the foot occur mainly at the:
- A. Ankle joint
- B. Inferior tibiofibular joint
- C. Superior tibiofibular joint
- D. Subtalar and other tarsal joints
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="The deltoid ligament is so strong that excessive tensile force more often avulses a piece of bone than tears the ligament. Medial ligament injuries are also less common than lateral ones and are usually associated with a fracture." %}
Compared with the lateral ligament, the deltoid ligament of the ankle:
- A. Is weaker and torn more often
- B. Is stronger, and excessive force tends to cause an avulsion fracture
- C. Has two bands
- D. Attaches to the lateral malleolus
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="At the front of the ankle, from medial to lateral, the order is tibialis anterior, extensor hallucis longus, anterior tibial vessels, deep fibular nerve, extensor digitorum longus, fibularis tertius. The vessels and the nerve lie between the two extensor tendons." %}
At the front of the ankle, what lies between the tendons of extensor hallucis longus and extensor digitorum longus?
- A. Tibialis anterior tendon
- B. Fibularis tertius tendon
- C. Anterior tibial vessels and deep fibular nerve
- D. Great saphenous vein and saphenous nerve
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="What type of joint is the ankle and which bones form it?" %}
A synovial hinge joint. It is formed by the lower end of the tibia with the medial malleolus, the lateral malleolus of the fibula, and the body of the talus. The inferior transverse tibiofibular ligament completes the socket.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Why is the ankle so stable, and why is dislocation rare?" %}
The talus is wedge-shaped and sits in a deep mortise formed by the malleoli, the tibia and the inferior transverse ligament. Strong collateral ligaments and the tendons crossing the joint add stability. When dislocation does occur, a malleolus is usually fractured as well.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Describe the deltoid ligament." %}
A strong triangular ligament on the medial side. Its apex and margins attach to the medial malleolus. Its fibres pass to the navicular and spring ligament (tibionavicular), the sustentaculum tali (tibiocalcanean) and the talus (tibiotalar parts). It resists eversion.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Name the parts of the lateral ligament and say which is torn most often." %}
The anterior talofibular, calcaneofibular and posterior talofibular ligaments. The anterior talofibular ligament is injured most often, in inversion sprains with the foot plantar flexed. The posterior talofibular ligament is almost always spared.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What movements occur at the ankle and which muscles produce them?" %}
Dorsiflexion by tibialis anterior, helped by extensor digitorum longus, extensor hallucis longus and fibularis tertius. Plantar flexion by gastrocnemius and soleus, helped by plantaris, tibialis posterior, flexor hallucis longus and flexor digitorum longus.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is the nerve supply of the ankle joint?" %}
The deep fibular, saphenous, sural and tibial nerves. Occasionally the superficial fibular nerve also supplies it.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is the inferior tibiofibular joint and what holds it?" %}
A syndesmosis, which is a fibrous joint with no capsule. It is held by the anterior, posterior, inferior transverse and interosseous ligaments. The interosseous ligament is the strongest union between the bones.
{% /viva %}

